The book gives me Theorem 3.12 (p. 92), Definition 3.5 (p. 71), and the series facts I need on p. 484 (Math Fact B.7 is exactly the arithmetic-geometric sum). Now I'll set up the problem and send every number through the tools: the expected overage sum, both plans' expected costs at several values of p, and probes to locate where the comparison flips.
