{"type":"hint","text":"Both plans bill the same random variable \\(M\\), so write each monthly cost as a constant plus a term in \\(M\\). For the old plan, mind the kink at 30 minutes: it decides what the expectation can and cannot pass through."}
{"type":"part","label":"(a)","title":"Expected cost of the new plan"}
{"type":"step","title":"Write C as a function of M"}
{"type":"para","text":"The new plan has no free allowance: $15 every month, then $1 for each minute, whether it is your first minute or your hundredth. The bill in dollars is the **linear** function"}
{"type":"math","tex":"C = 15 + M"}
{"type":"statement","kind":"theorem","number":"3.12","name":"Linearity of expectation","page":92,"text":"For any random variable \\(X\\), \\(\\mathrm{E}[aX + b] = a\\,\\mathrm{E}[X] + b\\)."}
{"type":"step","title":"Take expectations"}
{"type":"derivation","steps":[{"tex":"\\mathrm{E}[C] = \\mathrm{E}[15 + M]","why":"The bill is a linear function of the minute count."},{"tex":"= 15 + \\mathrm{E}[M]","why":"Theorem 3.12 with a = 1 and b = 15 [p. 92]."},{"tex":"= 15 + \\frac{1}{p}","why":"M is geometric with mean 1/p, as the problem states."}]}
{"type":"note","text":"Units check: the $15 term is dollars and the $1 rate is dollars per minute, so the rate times \\(M\\) minutes lands back in dollars. For the Problem 3.6.6 user, \\(\\mathrm{E}[M] = 30\\) minutes, and the expected bill under the new plan comes to $45."}
{"type":"answer","label":"(a)","text":"\\(\\mathrm{E}[C] = 15 + 1/p\\) dollars: a flat $15 plus the expected number of minutes."}
{"type":"part","label":"(b)","title":"When the new plan is preferable"}
{"type":"note","text":"One reading to settle first. Problem 3.6.6 pinned \\(p = 1/30\\) (\\(\\mathrm{E}[M] = 30\\)), but Problems 3.7.7 and 3.7.8 treat \\(p\\) as the free parameter of the same geometric \\(M\\). The question is which tariff is cheaper for a user with usage parameter \\(p\\), so both plans are compared at the same \\(p\\). If you instead pin \\(p = 1/30\\), there is nothing to vary: the old plan simply wins, about $25.42 against $45."}
{"type":"step","title":"Write the old plan as a function of M"}
{"type":"para","text":"Problem 3.6.6's plan charges $20 per month with the first 30 minutes included and $0.50 for each minute beyond 30; expressing that in terms of \\(M\\) is exactly Problem 3.7.7. With the convention \\((x)^{+} = \\max(x, 0)\\),"}
{"type":"math","tex":"C_{\\text{old}} = 20 + 0.5\\,(M-30)^{+}, \\qquad (x)^{+} = \\max(x,\\,0)"}
{"type":"para","text":"The \\({}^{+}\\) carries all the structure: for \\(M \\le 30\\) the bill is exactly $20, and each minute past 30 adds $0.50. That kink at 30 is what makes \\(C_{\\text{old}}\\) **nonlinear** in \\(M\\)."}
{"type":"callout","tone":"caveat","title":"Don't clip inside the expectation","text":"It is tempting to write \\(\\mathrm{E}[(M-30)^{+}] = (\\mathrm{E}[M] - 30)^{+}\\). That is wrong: \\(\\mathrm{E}[g(X)] = g(\\mathrm{E}[X])\\) holds only for linear \\(g\\), and the book itself warns that beyond Theorem 3.12 it is 'tempting, but usually wrong' to assume otherwise [p. 92]. A max has a kink, so the average must go through the PMF via Theorem 3.10. It is the same trap as Problem 3.7.6, \\(\\mathrm{E}[1/X] \\ne 1/\\mathrm{E}[X]\\)."}
{"type":"step","title":"Average the overage through the PMF"}
{"type":"para","text":"Theorem 3.10 [pp. 90–91] averages a derived random variable against the PMF of \\(M\\), which is geometric (p) by Definition 3.5 [p. 71]. Minutes \\(m \\le 30\\) contribute zero, so the sum starts at \\(m = 31\\)."}
{"type":"statement","kind":"math fact","number":"B.7","name":"Infinite series","page":484,"text":"If \\(|q| < 1\\), \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} i q^{i} = \\frac{q}{(1-q)^{2}}\\)."}
{"type":"derivation","steps":[{"tex":"\\mathrm{E}\\big[(M-30)^{+}\\big] = \\sum_{m=31}^{\\infty} (m-30)\\,p(1-p)^{m-1}","why":"Theorem 3.10 with \\(g(m) = (m-30)^{+}\\) and the geometric PMF; minutes at or below 30 add nothing."},{"tex":"= p(1-p)^{30} \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} k(1-p)^{k-1}","why":"Substitute \\(k = m - 30\\), so each term is \\(k\\,p(1-p)^{k+29}\\), and factor out the constant \\(p(1-p)^{30}\\)."},{"tex":"= \\frac{p(1-p)^{30}}{\\big(1-(1-p)\\big)^{2}}","why":"Math Fact B.7 [p. 484] with \\(q = 1-p\\), divided through by \\(q\\), gives \\(\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} k q^{k-1} = 1/(1-q)^{2}\\)."},{"tex":"= \\frac{(1-p)^{30}}{p}","why":"The denominator is \\(1 - (1-p) = p\\), which cancels one power of \\(p\\)."}]}
{"type":"math","tex":"\\mathrm{E}[C_{\\text{old}}] = 20 + \\frac{(1-p)^{30}}{2p}"}
{"type":"note","text":"Sanity check at \\(p = 1/30\\), the Problem 3.6.6 user: \\(\\mathrm{E}[C_{\\text{old}}] = 20 + 15(29/30)^{30} \\approx 25.42\\), the $20 base plus about $5.42 of expected overage. The factor \\((1-p)^{30} = \\mathrm{P}[M > 30]\\) is the chance you even run past the free allowance, and it dies off fast as \\(p\\) grows."}
{"type":"step","title":"Set the two expected bills against each other"}
{"type":"para","text":"The new plan is preferable when its expected bill is the smaller one. Since \\(p > 0\\), multiplying both sides by \\(2p\\) is safe."}
{"type":"derivation","steps":[{"tex":"15 + \\frac{1}{p} \\;<\\; 20 + \\frac{(1-p)^{30}}{2p}","why":"New plan preferable: the two expected bills at the same \\(p\\)."},{"tex":"30p + 2 \\;<\\; 40p + (1-p)^{30}","why":"Multiply both sides by \\(2p > 0\\)."},{"tex":"2 - 10p \\;<\\; (1-p)^{30}","why":"Collect the \\(p\\) terms on the left."}]}
{"type":"step","title":"Read off the winning values of p"}
{"type":"para","text":"If \\(p \\ge 0.2\\), the left side \\(2 - 10p\\) is zero or negative while \\((1-p)^{30}\\) is positive, so the inequality holds on its own: the new plan is strictly cheaper. For \\(p < 0.2\\) both sides are positive and the race is close. The new plan wins exactly when \\(f(p) = (1-p)^{30} + 10p - 2 > 0\\), and numeric probes bracket the sign change:"}
{"type":"table","columns":["p","f(p)","Verdict"],"rows":[["0.1","-0.958","Old plan cheaper"],["0.19","-0.098","Old plan cheaper"],["0.1998","-0.00075","Old plan cheaper"],["0.1999","+0.00024","New plan cheaper"],["0.2","+0.00124","New plan cheaper"]]}
{"type":"note","text":"The crossover sits between \\(p = 0.1998\\) and \\(p = 0.1999\\), a hair below 0.2. At any practical precision the break-even is 0.2, which is why the answer is stated as \\(p \\ge 0.2\\)."}
{"type":"table","columns":["p","E[M] (minutes)","New plan ($)","Old plan ($)","Cheaper plan"],"rows":[["1/30","30","45","25.42","Old plan"],["0.1","10","25","20.21","Old plan"],["1/6","6","21","20.01","Old plan"],["0.2","5","20","20.003","New plan"],["0.25","4","19","20.0004","New plan"]]}
{"type":"callout","tone":"insight","title":"Why five minutes is the break-even","text":"The new plan saves exactly $5 of base fee, $15 against $20, but charges twice as much, $1 against $0.50, for every minute past the allowance. So everything hinges on expected overage, and \\((1-p)^{30} = \\mathrm{P}[M > 30]\\) collapses quickly as \\(p\\) grows: by \\(p = 0.2\\) the old plan's expected overage, the 0.003 in the table, is about a third of a cent. Light users never reach the 30 free minutes, so they pocket the $5. Heavy users pay dearly for the allowance: at \\(\\mathrm{E}[M] = 30\\), the old plan costs about $25.42 against $45."}
{"type":"answer","label":"(b)","text":"The new plan is preferable for \\(p \\ge 0.2\\), equivalently when the expected usage \\(\\mathrm{E}[M] = 1/p\\) is at most 5 minutes per month; the exact numerical break-even is \\(p \\approx 0.1999\\). At the Problem 3.6.6 usage level \\(p = 1/30\\), the old plan is far cheaper: about $25.42 per month against $45."}